<!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <!-- SECTION 1: ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS                                      -->
  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <section id="core-foundations" class="content-block">
    <div class="section-tag-wrap">
      <span class="badge badge-accent">SECTION 1</span>
      <span class="badge badge-yellow">MICROARCHITECTURAL THEORY</span>
    </div>
    <h2 class="block-title">1. Architectural Foundations: Anatomy of Vector &amp; Coprocessor Engines</h2>
    <p class="block-lead">
      Scalar microarchitectures fundamentally saturate their execution efficiency at 1 instruction per cycle per execution unit due to instruction fetch, decode, and register renaming overheads. Vector and coprocessor engines bypass these von Neumann bottlenecks by amortizing a single decoded instruction over dozens to thousands of arithmetic operations across space (parallel execution lanes) and time (multi-cycle vector register expansion).
    </p>

    <!-- 1.1 Seven Universal Subsystems Grid -->
    <h3 class="subsection-title">1.1 The Seven Universal Subsystems of Vector Accelerators</h3>
    <p class="sub-lead">
      Every production-grade vector coprocessor—regardless of whether it targets 35 kGE microcontroller DSP or 1 MGE datacenter compute—is composed of seven tightly coordinated hardware subsystems:
    </p>

    <div class="subsystems-grid">
      <div class="subsystem-card">
        <div class="subsystem-num">01</div>
        <div class="subsystem-body">
          <h4 class="subsystem-name">Host-Coprocessor Issue &amp; Instruction Queue</h4>
          <p class="subsystem-desc">
            Asynchronous decoupled command queue bridging the host scalar pipeline (e.g., CVA6, Snitch, Rocket) and the coprocessor. Tracks data dependencies between scalar GPR operands and vector register configurations (<code>vsetvl</code>) while buffering burst issues.
          </p>
          <div class="subsystem-tag">OBI / RoCC / Coprocessor FIFO</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="subsystem-card">
        <div class="subsystem-num">02</div>
        <div class="subsystem-body">
          <h4 class="subsystem-name">Vector Sequencer &amp; Element Expander</h4>
          <p class="subsystem-desc">
            Autonomous control FSM expanding high-level vector instructions (<code>vadd.vv</code>, <code>vfmacc.vv</code>) into multi-cycle micro-operations across lanes based on active vector length (<code>vl</code>), standard element width (<code>SEW</code>), and register group multiplier (<code>LMUL</code>).
          </p>
          <div class="subsystem-tag">Multi-Cycle FSM &bull; Hazard Scoreboard</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="subsystem-card">
        <div class="subsystem-num">03</div>
        <div class="subsystem-body">
          <h4 class="subsystem-name">Multi-Banked Vector Register File (VRF)</h4>
          <p class="subsystem-desc">
            High-density register memory (512 to 4096 bits per vector register) physically partitioned into orthogonal SRAM or latch banks (e.g., 8–16 banks per lane) enabling simultaneous conflict-free reads for 2–3 source operands and concurrent writeback.
          </p>
          <div class="subsystem-tag">8–16 Banks/Lane &bull; Multi-Port Virtualization</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="subsystem-card">
        <div class="subsystem-num">04</div>
        <div class="subsystem-body">
          <h4 class="subsystem-name">Parallel Execution Lanes (SIMD Slices)</h4>
          <p class="subsystem-desc">
            Replicated heterogeneous computing slices operating concurrently. Each lane integrates a 64-bit integer ALU/shifter, IEEE-754 multi-precision floating-point units (FP64/FP32/FP16), and fused multiply-accumulators (FMA) providing 2 to 4 FLOPS/cycle per lane.
          </p>
          <div class="subsystem-tag">SIMD FPU &bull; INT ALU &bull; 64b Datapath</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="subsystem-card">
        <div class="subsystem-num">05</div>
        <div class="subsystem-body">
          <h4 class="subsystem-name">Slide, Permute &amp; Reduction Network</h4>
          <p class="subsystem-desc">
            Crossbar switching fabric routing elements across adjacent lanes for spatial data rearrangement instructions including <code>vslideup</code>, <code>vslidedown</code>, <code>vrgather</code>, and tree-based parallel reduction folds (<code>vfredsum</code>).
          </p>
          <div class="subsystem-tag">Crossbar Cross-Lane Interconnect</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="subsystem-card">
        <div class="subsystem-num">06</div>
        <div class="subsystem-body">
          <h4 class="subsystem-name">Vector Load-Store Unit (VLSU) &amp; AGU</h4>
          <p class="subsystem-desc">
            Massive bandwidth memory interface orchestrating unit-stride contiguous bursts, constant-stride skips, and non-contiguous gather/scatter indexed transfers with outstanding miss-handling buffers and address alignment crossbars.
          </p>
          <div class="subsystem-tag">Unit-Stride Burst &bull; Strided/Indexed AGU</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="subsystem-card">
        <div class="subsystem-num">07</div>
        <div class="subsystem-body">
          <h4 class="subsystem-name">Wide System Interconnect Highway</h4>
          <p class="subsystem-desc">
            Ultra-wide on-chip interface (128-bit to 512-bit AXI4 or TileLink-C) connecting the VLSU directly to shared L2 SRAM clusters, multi-banked scratchpads, or high-bandwidth DRAM controllers to prevent compute starvation.
          </p>
          <div class="subsystem-tag">128b–512b AXI4 / TileLink Interconnect</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

