    <!-- 1.2 Execution Paradigms: Top-Down Progressive Ladder -->
    <h3 class="subsection-title">1.2 Execution Paradigms: From 1D Vectors to 2D Spatial &amp; SIMT Acceleration</h3>
    <p class="sub-lead">
      Accelerating high-density linear algebra divides open-source silicon into four distinct computational execution paradigms organized in progressive order of spatial specialization:
    </p>

    <div class="paradigm-ladder-flow">
      <!-- Paradigm 1 -->
      <div class="paradigm-step-card">
        <div class="paradigm-step-head bg-yellow">
          <span class="p-badge">PARADIGM 1 &bull; 1D DYNAMIC VECTORIZATION</span>
          <h4 class="p-title">RISC-V Vector 1.0 (RVV) Coprocessors (Ara, AraXL, Spatz, Vicuna)</h4>
        </div>
        <div class="paradigm-body">
          <p>
            <strong>Vector-Length Agnostic (VLA) Programming:</strong> Binaries compile once and run unmodified across any hardware vector length (\(VLEN \in [128, 4096]\)) and lane count (\(1 \le N \le 16\)). The application configures <code>vl</code> via <code>vsetvli</code> and loops through arbitrary tensor dimensions without recompilation.
          </p>
          <ul class="paradigm-points">
            <li><strong>Strengths:</strong> Extreme mathematical flexibility—executes non-linear activations (Softmax, GELU, Exp), FFT permutations, arbitrary convolutions, and general scientific computing.</li>
            <li><strong>Limitations:</strong> Register file and crossbar area scale aggressively. High-density GEMM requires continuous register streaming, leaving utilization vulnerable to scalar host issue bubbles.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 1 -> 2 -->
      <div class="boom-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Specialize Datapath from 1D Multi-Lane SIMD to 2D Weight-Stationary Systolic Mesh (+360 kGE)</span>
      </div>

      <!-- Paradigm 2 -->
      <div class="paradigm-step-card">
        <div class="paradigm-step-head bg-teal">
          <span class="p-badge">PARADIGM 2 &bull; 2D SPATIAL SYSTOLIC ACCELERATION</span>
          <h4 class="p-title">2D Spatial Systolic Mesh Engines (Gemmini 16&times;16)</h4>
        </div>
        <div class="paradigm-body">
          <p>
            <strong>Rigid 2D Matrix Compute Mesh:</strong> Physical 2D processing element (PE) grid executing dense matrix multiplies with spatial data reuse. Weights or output partial sums remain stationary inside PE accumulator latches while activations stream across orthogonal rows and columns.
          </p>
          <ul class="paradigm-points">
            <li><strong>Strengths:</strong> Maximizes arithmetic density per square millimeter—achieves up to <strong>450.8 Ops/cycle</strong> (88% utilization) on dense GEMM by reusing operands locally without register file traffic.</li>
            <li><strong>Limitations:</strong> Completely rigid datapath. Incapable of executing arbitrary math functions, reductions, or non-unit stride permutations without transferring data back to the host CPU.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 2 -> 3 -->
      <div class="boom-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Decouple Memory Traversal via Autonomous Hardware Stream Generators (SSR / DAE)</span>
      </div>

      <!-- Paradigm 3 -->
      <div class="paradigm-step-card">
        <div class="paradigm-step-head bg-light-blue">
          <span class="p-badge">PARADIGM 3 &bull; DECOUPLED ACCESS-EXECUTE (DAE)</span>
          <h4 class="p-title">Autonomous Streaming Accelerators (Snitch SSR &amp; Hwacha Decoupled Queues)</h4>
        </div>
        <div class="paradigm-body">
          <p>
            <strong>Address-Compute Decoupling:</strong> Dedicated hardware address generation units (AGUs) traverse multi-dimensional data arrays autonomously and stream elements directly into computation registers, hiding hundreds of cycles of memory latency behind computation.
          </p>
          <ul class="paradigm-points">
            <li><strong>Strengths:</strong> Sustains near 100% compute lane saturation on irregular memory strides; immune to scalar host issue bandwidth collapse.</li>
            <li><strong>Limitations:</strong> Requires custom ISA extensions or streaming semantic registers (SSR), limiting portable software ecosystem integration.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 3 -> 4 -->
      <div class="boom-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Transition from Explicit Vector/Matrix ISA to Implicit SIMT Thread Warps (+1,240 kGE)</span>
      </div>

      <!-- Paradigm 4 -->
      <div class="paradigm-step-card">
        <div class="paradigm-step-head bg-cream">
          <span class="p-badge">PARADIGM 4 &bull; MANY-CORE SIMT GPGPU EXECUTION</span>
          <h4 class="p-title">Fine-Grained Thread Warp Coprocessors (Vortex RISC-V GPGPU)</h4>
        </div>
        <div class="paradigm-body">
          <p>
            <strong>Single Instruction Multiple Threads (SIMT):</strong> Hardware warp schedulers interleave execution across dozens of independent thread warps, dynamically masking stalled threads on memory misses and managing control divergence via hardware divergence stacks.
          </p>
          <ul class="paradigm-points">
            <li><strong>Strengths:</strong> Standard OpenCL / CUDA programming model; massive memory latency tolerance through multi-warp interleaving; flexible control flow.</li>
            <li><strong>Limitations:</strong> Substantially higher silicon area (1.65 MGE per cluster) and static leakage due to warp scoreboards, operand collectors, and large thread register files.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
<!-- 1.3 The Arithmetic Density Triad (Top-Down Progressive Flow) -->
    <h3 class="subsection-title" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">1.3 The Arithmetic Density Triad (Top-Down Progressive Flow)</h3>
    <p class="sub-lead">
      Silicon architects navigate three interdependent design axes when sizing vector accelerators, trading silicon gate budgets against memory bandwidth and dataflow specialization:
    </p>

    <div class="triad-vertical-flow">
      <!-- Axis 1 -->
      <div class="triad-card bg-cream">
        <div class="triad-head">
          <span class="t-badge badge-green">AXIS 1 &bull; COMPACT EMBEDDED</span>
          <h4 class="t-title">Lane Count &amp; Datapath Width (Spatz / Vicuna ~40 kGE)</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="t-desc">
          Single-lane embedded vector coprocessor coupled to lightweight microcontrollers (Snitch, CV32E40P, Ibex). Eliminates crossbar routing entirely; single-port latch VRF provides 1–2 FLOPS/cycle at minimal dynamic energy, ideal for IoT edge inference.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 1 -> 2 -->
      <div class="flow-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Scale SIMD Datapath to Standard RVV 1.0 Multi-Lane (+180 kGE Silicon Investment)</span>
      </div>

      <!-- Axis 2 -->
      <div class="triad-card bg-light-blue">
        <div class="triad-head">
          <span class="t-badge badge-blue">AXIS 2 &bull; SCALABLE RVV 1.0</span>
          <h4 class="t-title">VRF Multi-Banking &amp; Crossbar Contention (Ara / AraXL ~220&ndash;340 kGE)</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="t-desc">
          Scales to 4&ndash;16 parallel execution lanes with 4096-bit vector registers. Multi-banked VRFs (8&ndash;16 banks per lane) and wide 128b&ndash;512b AXI4 crossbars prevent memory stalls, sustaining up to 16 FLOPS/cycle on dense GEMM.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 2 -> 3 -->
      <div class="flow-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Transition from 1D Vector Registers to 2D Spatial Systolic Mesh (+240 kGE Density Expansion)</span>
      </div>

      <!-- Axis 3 -->
      <div class="triad-card bg-light-teal">
        <div class="triad-head">
          <span class="t-badge badge-teal">AXIS 3 &bull; 2D SPATIAL SYSTOLIC</span>
          <h4 class="t-title">Dedicated Spatial Matrix Acceleration (Gemmini ~580 kGE)</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="t-desc">
          Replaces 1D vector register files with a rigid 2D grid of 256 multiply-accumulate PEs. Delivers 354+ Ops/cycle on quantized neural network workloads via autonomous scratchpad DMA transfers and weight-stationary reuse.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 1.4 The 8-Archetype Architectural Spectrum (Top-Down Flow) -->
    <h3 class="subsection-title" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">1.4 The 8-Archetype Architectural Spectrum &amp; Phase Transitions</h3>
    <p class="sub-lead">
      SCORE establishes the first unified taxonomy organizing all open-source hardware accelerators into eight progressive microarchitectural archetypes across three computational domains:
    </p>

    <div class="spectrum-domain-wrap">
      <!-- Domain A -->
      <div class="domain-header">
        <span class="badge badge-yellow">DOMAIN A: 1D SUB-WORD &amp; VECTOR DATAPATHS</span>
        <span class="d-sub">Sequential vector register files with linear lane scaling</span>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 1</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Packed Sub-Word SIMD (Zero-VRF / DSP Extension)</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Slices existing 32-bit or 64-bit integer registers into 2&times;16-bit or 4&times;8-bit sub-words. Features zero architectural vector state; uses standard scalar pipeline ALU and GPRs for low-cost DSP (e.g. CV32E40P <code>pv.dotsp.b</code>, RISC-V P-Extension).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~15&ndash;30 kGE &bull; Peak: 4 MACs/cycle (INT8)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 1 -> 2 -->
      <div class="boom-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Phase Transition: Zero-VRF to Dedicated Vector Register File (+15 kGE Silicon Investment)</span>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 2</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Compact Embedded Vector Coprocessor (Zve32 / Zve64)</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Ultra-compact standard RVV coprocessor (1&ndash;2 lanes, VLEN=128&ndash;512b) coupled to embedded scalar cores via low-latency dual-port interfaces. Prioritizes minimal area and latch-based VRFs for quantized edge ML (e.g. Spatz, Vicuna).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~35&ndash;65 kGE &bull; Peak: 1&ndash;2 Ops/cycle (FP64) / 6.4 Ops/cycle (INT8)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 2 -> 3 -->
      <div class="boom-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Scale Datapath: Multi-Cycle Element Folding to Full Multi-Lane SIMD (+160 kGE Silicon Investment)</span>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 3</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Standard Scalable Monolithic RVV 1.0 (4&ndash;16 Lanes)</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Full-scale vector coprocessor implementing ratified RVV 1.0 with wide multi-lane SIMD datapaths, multi-banked SRAM VRFs, and wide AXI4 memory interfaces (e.g. Ara, 4&ndash;16 lanes, VLEN=4096b).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~220&ndash;850 kGE &bull; Peak: 8&ndash;32 Ops/cycle (FP64)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 3 -> 4 -->
      <div class="boom-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Topology Shift: Monolithic O(N^2) Crossbar to Clustered Multi-Bank VRF (+120 kGE Silicon Investment)</span>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 4</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Clustered Multi-Bank VRF Scale-Up</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Decoupled multi-cluster vector units with hierarchical inter-lane routing. Eliminates monolithic crossbar wire congestion by clustering lanes into local banking groups (e.g. AraXL 8-lane dual-cluster with 64 SRAM banks).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~340&ndash;1,200 kGE &bull; Peak: 16&ndash;64 Ops/cycle (FP64)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Domain B -->
      <div class="domain-header" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <span class="badge badge-teal">DOMAIN B: STREAMING &amp; 2D SPATIAL ACCELERATORS</span>
        <span class="d-sub">Decoupled access-execute &amp; spatial systolic PE meshes</span>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 4 -> 5 -->
      <div class="boom-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Decoupling Shift: Synchronous Issue Queue to Autonomous Hardware AGU / SSR Stream (+70 kGE)</span>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 5</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Decoupled Access-Execute (DAE) Streaming Engine</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Separates memory access from arithmetic execution. Dedicated address engines push data streams into decoupled queues or Stream Semantic Registers (SSR), hiding hundreds of cycles of DRAM latency (e.g. Snitch Cluster, Hwacha).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~70&ndash;410 kGE &bull; Peak: 8&ndash;16 Ops/cycle (DAE Streaming)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 5 -> 6 -->
      <div class="boom-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Dimensional Shift: 1D Element Vectorization to 2D Spatial Systolic Mesh (+170 kGE)</span>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 6</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">2D Spatial Systolic Matrix Engine</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Two-dimensional processing element (PE) mesh executing outer-product or weight-stationary matrix multiply-accumulate operations with spatial data reuse, maximizing MACs per milliwatt (e.g. Gemmini 16&times;16 mesh).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~450&ndash;1,100 kGE &bull; Peak: 256&ndash;512 MACs/cycle (INT8)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Domain C -->
      <div class="domain-header" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <span class="badge badge-coral">DOMAIN C: HYBRID TENSORS &amp; MULTI-THREADED GPGPUS</span>
        <span class="d-sub">Matrix tile extensions and fine-grained SIMT execution</span>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 6 -> 7 -->
      <div class="boom-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Hybridization: Dedicated Systolic Mesh to Dynamic 2D Outer-Product Matrix Tiles (-270 kGE)</span>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 7</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Vector-Matrix Hybrid &amp; 2D Tile Tensor Engine</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Unifies 1D vector instructions with 2D matrix tile extensions (e.g. RISC-V SME / IME, TVM VTA), allowing outer-product matrix multiplication to execute directly on 2D accumulator registers with transpose networks.
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~280&ndash;620 kGE &bull; Peak: 64&ndash;128 Ops/cycle (INT8/FP16)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 7 -> 8 -->
      <div class="boom-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Execution Shift: Explicit Vector/Matrix ISA to Implicit SIMT Thread Warps (+1,340 kGE)</span>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 8</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Many-Core SIMT (GPGPU Coprocessor)</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Massively parallel GPU-style coprocessor executing OpenCL/CUDA kernels via hardware warp schedulers, divergence handling stacks, and clustered SIMT core pipelines (e.g. Vortex RISC-V GPGPU).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~1,200&ndash;3,500 kGE &bull; Peak: 16&ndash;64 Ops/cycle (SIMT FP32)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


