    <!-- 3.2 Unified Coprocessor Interfaces & Memory Hierarchies -->
    <h3 class="subsection-title">3.2 Unified Coprocessor Interfaces &amp; Memory Hierarchies</h3>
    <p class="sub-lead">
      Connecting an accelerator to a host RISC-V scalar core requires balancing issue queue latency, hazard tracking, and memory coherence:
    </p>

    <div class="arch-features-grid">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <span class="f-badge">ROCKET / BOOM ECOSYSTEM</span>
        <h3>Rocket Custom Coprocessor (RoCC)</h3>
        <p>
          Utilized by Gemmini and Hwacha. Provides non-blocking command issue, scalar argument passing (<code>rs1</code>, <code>rs2</code>), autonomous memory translation access (via host PTW), and decoupled completion response queues.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-card">
        <span class="f-badge">PULP PLATFORM STANDARD</span>
        <h3>Open Bus Interface (OBI)</h3>
        <p>
          Utilized by Spatz, CV32E40P, and Snitch. Point-to-point, single-cycle latency synchronous interface supporting back-to-back word transfers without address-data phase serialization. Minimizes area in microcontroller clusters.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-card">
        <span class="f-badge">CVA6 / CHESHIRE INTEGRATION</span>
        <h3>CVA6 Coprocessor Interface</h3>
        <p>
          Utilized by Ara and AraXL. Asynchronous decoupled channel passing 32-bit vector instruction words alongside scalar register values. Tracks instruction retirement and precise exceptions via in-order scoreboard handshakes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-card">
        <span class="f-badge">SYSTEM INTERCONNECT</span>
        <h3>AXI4 &amp; TileLink-C Memory Highway</h3>
        <p>
          Connects wide vector load-store units directly to multi-banked L2 caches or high-bandwidth DRAM controllers (128-bit to 512-bit width), delivering up to 64 bytes per clock cycle to sustain lane computing saturation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 3.3 The Open Silicon Gaps Callout -->
    <div class="callout callout-warning" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <div class="callout-header">
        <span class="badge badge-accent">ARCHITECTURAL GAP AUDIT</span>
        <h3 class="callout-title">The Open Silicon Gaps: Matrix Tile Extensions &amp; GPGPU Toolchains</h3>
      </div>
      <p>
        While 1D standard RVV coprocessors (Ara, Spatz, Vicuna) and 2D systolic arrays (Gemmini) have mature silicon-proven open-source hardware, the open silicon ecosystem exhibits two critical frontiers:
      </p>
      <ul style="margin: 0.5rem 0 0.5rem 1.25rem; font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.55;">
        <li><strong>Open Matrix Tile Extensions (Archetype 7):</strong> While commercial designs (SiFive Intelligence, Alibaba XuanTie C920) tape out 2D tile matrix registers (ZA tile registers), open-source implementations remain largely confined to standardization working drafts (<code>integrated-matrix-extension</code>) and QEMU/Spike software models. Fully open, synthesizable RTL tensor engines remain an active research gap.</li>
        <li><strong>Open-Source GPGPU Toolchains (Archetype 8):</strong> Vortex GPGPU represents a breakthrough full-stack open-source SIMT GPGPU. However, synthesizing a 4-core cluster exceeds 1.5 MGE, requiring substantial FPGA/ASIC memory infrastructure (GDDR6 / HBM models via Ramulator). Bridging lightweight RVV vector cores and heavy GPGPU SIMT pipelines represents the next decade of open hardware innovation.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

